adows, and the artificial lighting is diffuse and multi-directional.\n- The absence of long, directional shadows (which would be present in late afternoon) further confirms the sun has set.\n\n---\n\n**4. Illumination of Street Lights and Neon Signs:**\n- All street lights and neon signs (e.g., McDonald’s, Wolfgang Puck, the large “Takeshita Street” sign) are **fully illuminated** and appear bright against the dark sky.\n- This is typical behavior for commercial districts in Japan, where lights turn on at dusk to attract customers.\n- The signs are not dim or flickering, and there is no indication of them being turned off for the night — which would happen closer to **midnight**.\n- The vibrancy of the lights suggests it’s still early in the evening when foot traffic and business activity are high.\n\n---\n\n**5. Overall Atmosphere:**\n- The streets are **crowded with pedestrians**, indicating peak evening hours, not the quiet of late night or the sparseness of early morning.\n- The narrator’s captions mention being “now in Harajuku from Shibuya” and “not yet in the mood for street food,” implying a transition period — consistent with early evening after arriving from another location.\n- The energy is lively and bustling, which aligns with Harajuku’s reputation as a nightlife and youth culture hub that peaks in the **evening hours**.\n\n---\n\n**Distinguishing Between Evening and Midnight:**\n- **Midnight** would feature a **completely black sky**, potentially with stars or moonlight, and significantly **fewer pedestrians**.\n- Neon signs might still be on, but the streets would be quieter, and some shops may be closing or dimming lights.\n- In these frames, the **crowds are dense**, the **lights are fully blazing**, and the **sky retains a twilight hue** — all strong indicators of **early evening**, not midnight.\n\n---\n\n✅ **Conclusion: The time of day is evening — specifically, dusk transitioning into early night, approximately 6:00 PM to 8:00 PM.**'}], 'format