202f0975‑1000 – Takeshita\u202fStreet, Harajuku)**  \n\n- **Time of day:**\u202fEvening – dusk moving into early night (≈\u202f6:00‑8:00\u202fPM). The sky shifts from a deep blue‑grey (frames\u202f0980‑0985) to a solid navy (frame\u202f0990), indicating civil twilight just after sunset.  \n\n- **Lighting:**\u202fNatural light has faded; artificial illumination dominates. Neon signs, shop fronts (e.g., McDonald’s, Wolfgang\u202fPuck) and street lamps are fully lit and vivid, with no dimming or flickering that would suggest midnight.  \n\n- **Shadows:**\u202fVirtually absent; diffuse, multi‑directional artificial light prevents clear, long shadows.  \n\n- **Atmosphere & Activity:**\u202fThe street is densely packed with pedestrians—young shoppers, small groups, and individual passers‑by—typical of Harajuku’s early‑evening rush. The lively crowd and bright storefronts signal that businesses are still in full swing, contrasting with the quieter, darker ambience of late‑night.  \n\n- **Character tracking:**\u202fAcross the six frames, the camera follows a steady flow of people moving left‑to‑right along the promenade. No single individual remains centered for more than one frame, but the collective motion (clusters forming near intersections and dispersing toward exits) provides a continuous sense of bustling foot traffic.  \n\n**Conclusion:** The visual cues across frames\u202f0975‑1000 depict an early‑evening scene on Takeshita\u202fStreet—twilight sky, bright neon, and a heavy, lively pedestrian presence—clearly distinguishing it from both morning and midnight settings.', 'failed_images': []}
